# <% .Name %>

Go API on clean architecture, built on the shared kit (`<% .KitModule %>`). **Nothing runs outside Docker**,
not even locally: every command below starts a container. `make` lists all targets.

```bash
make init            # .env with a fresh JWT secret, shared Go cache volume
make up              # API with hot reload on http://127.0.0.1:8080 + PostgreSQL
make migrate-up      # create the schema (migrations/ holds goose files, embedded in the migrate binary)
curl -s localhost:8080/healthz
make test            # unit tests;  make test-integration  needs the db container;  make check  = everything CI runs
```

## Layout (dependencies point inwards only, enforced by `internal/architecture_test.go`)

```
cmd/api            entrypoint: config -> bootstrap.Run; `api healthcheck` for the container healthcheck
cmd/migrate        one-shot migration command (up | down | status | create)
internal/domain    entities + repository interfaces. Imports nothing from the other layers.
internal/usecase   application rules (account: register/login/refresh, users: list/get). Depends on interfaces.
internal/adapter   httpapi (HTTP in) and postgres (SQL out). Implements / calls the layers above.
internal/bootstrap composition root: the only place that wires concrete types together
internal/config    environment variables -> typed Config
migrations         goose SQL files, embedded in the migrate binary
deploy/compose.yaml  production compose file for the shared infrastructure (used by scripts/ship.sh)
deploy/grafana/      the project's own Grafana dashboards and how its metrics reach them (see its README)
```

Add a feature by growing the layers from the inside: entity + repository interface in `domain`, the rule in
`usecase`, SQL in `adapter/postgres`, routes in `adapter/httpapi`, one line each in `bootstrap`.

## What the kit gives this project

| Need | Where |
|---|---|
| Filtering, sorting, pagination | `query` + `postgres.Paginate`; clients send `?filter[role]=admin&filter[email][prefix]=ada&sort=-created_at&page=2&page_size=50` |
| Allow / deny lists for fields | struct tags (`query:"-"`, `nofilter`, `nosort`), `query.WithFilterPolicy(query.DenyFields(...))`, per request `schema.Restrict(...)` |
| Caching, read models | `redisx` (`Remember`, `ReadModel`, versioned invalidation) as an opt-in **decorator** in `internal/adapter`; see "Caching" in `gokit/README.md`. Not enabled by default: wrap only the repositories and methods you choose |
| Revocable tokens | set `REDIS_URL` + `REDIS_KEY_PREFIX` and `POST /v1/auth/logout` appears (`auth.Revocations` on Redis); without Redis tokens simply expire |
| Stateless JWT auth | `auth` (HS256 or Ed25519, key rotation, access + refresh tokens), `httpx.Authenticate`, `httpx.RequireRoles` |
| Passwords | `auth.Argon2id` (self-upgrading hashes) |
| Database access | `orm` (typed CRUD, query builder, relations preloaded without N+1, bulk insert, soft delete, keyset pagination) on `postgres` (PgBouncer-safe pool, retry on start, slow-query log, transactions via `db.WithTx`) |
| Migrations | `migrate` (no session locks, safe behind PgBouncer) **and generated from the models**: `make migrate-diff NAME=...` |
| Tracing + metrics | `telemetry` (OTLP to the collector, no app-side sampling) + `httpx.Instrument` (`http.server.request.duration`) |
| Logs | `logging` (JSON, with `trace_id`) |
| Errors | `apperr` kinds -> HTTP status in `httpx`; internal errors are logged, never shown |

Filter operators: `eq ne gt gte lt lte like prefix in nin between null`. Filterable fields of `users`:
`id email name role created_at`; the password hash is not queryable.

## API

| Route | Auth |
|---|---|
| `POST /v1/auth/register` `{email,password,name}` | none |
| `POST /v1/auth/login` `{email,password}` | none |
| `POST /v1/auth/refresh` `{refresh_token}` | refresh token |
| `POST /v1/auth/logout` `{refresh_token?, everywhere?}` | access token; only when Redis is configured |
| `GET /v1/me` | access token |
| `GET /v1/users`, `GET /v1/users/{id}` | access token, role `admin` |
| `GET /healthz` (liveness), `GET /readyz` (dependencies) | none |

Make someone an admin: `make psql`, then `UPDATE users SET role='admin' WHERE email='you@example.com';`
(tokens issued earlier keep the old role until the next refresh).

Access tokens live 15 minutes; refresh tokens carry identity only, and the role is re-read from the database when
they are exchanged. Without Redis nothing can be revoked before expiry (stateless by design); with Redis
`logout` revokes the access token (and optionally the refresh token), and `{"everywhere": true}` voids every token
the user holds.

## Changing the schema

Edit the row struct in `internal/adapter/postgres` (tags describe the column), then:

```bash
make migrate-diff NAME=add_phone     # writes migrations/<timestamp>_add_phone.sql with Up AND Down; read its WARNING comments
make migrate-up                      # apply; make migrate-down rolls the last one back
make migrate-drift                   # CI: fails when the models changed without a migration
```

`migrate diff` compares the models with what your existing migrations actually produce (it applies them to a
temporary database), so it never relies on the state of your dev database. New row structs go into `Models()` in
`models.go`. Relations (`orm:"rel=belongs_to;fk=author_id"`, `has_many`, `many_to_many`) become foreign keys and join
tables. It never drops anything by itself (`FLAGS=--allow-drop` opts in). Beyond plain columns it handles:

* **Renames**: tag the field `orm:"was=old_column"` (table: a `WasTable() string` method) and the migration renames,
  keeping the data and the constraints and indexes attached. Remove the tag after the migration was applied.
* **CHECK constraints**: `orm:"check=age >= 0"` on a column, or a `Checks() map[string]string` method for table-level
  ones. They are matched by name, so give a changed check a new name.
* **Big production tables**: `make migrate-diff NAME=add_index FLAGS=--concurrent-indexes` puts new indexes on existing
  tables into a second, non-transactional file using `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY` (no write lock).

Joins in queries: `Has("Author", orm.Eq("name", "x"))` / `NotHas` (EXISTS, never repeats rows), `Join("Author")` and
`LeftJoin` with `"Author.name"` in `Where`/`OrderBy`, `orm.Project` for aggregates and joined columns, and
`orm.Fetch` for any SQL you want to write yourself, scanned by column name into a struct.

## The kit copy

`.kit/` is a vendored copy of the kit (`replace` in `go.mod`), so the project builds before the kit is
published. To follow kit changes run `make gokit-sync PROJECT=<this dir>` in the infrastructure repo. To use a
published version instead, delete `.kit/` and the `replace` line and `require` a tag.

## First deploy

1. In the infrastructure repo: `make new-project` (creates the DB, nginx blocks and `<apps dir>/<% .Name %>/.env`).
2. The generated env file already has `DATABASE_URL`, `REDIS_URL`, `REDIS_KEY_PREFIX` and `AMQP_URL`. Add the JWT settings to it: `JWT_SECRET=<64 hex chars>` (or `JWT_PRIVATE_KEY`), optionally
   `JWT_ISSUER`, `JWT_AUDIENCE`, `CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS`. `make gokit-jwt-key` in the infra repo prints keys.
3. `make ship` (vendors modules, builds `<% .Name %>-api:<git-sha>`, deploys with rollback). The compose file runs
   `<% .Name %>-migrate` (one-shot) before `<% .Name %>-api`.

`AGENTS.md` is the infrastructure contract (ports, limits, health, telemetry rules) this project follows.
